WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Welcome back.

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:06.294
A banked turn is a lovely example of one diagram doing more work than a pile

00:00:06.294 --> 00:00:06.420
of

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:06.880
memorized equations.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.340
We already found the frictionless speed.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.780
Now let's find the allowed range when static friction is available.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.700
This is an ideal particle model, not driving advice.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.180
The road rises toward the outside.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.580
Choose horizontal inward and vertical upward axes.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.540
The normal reaction points upward and inward.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.960
At a speed above the frictionless value, the car tends to slip outward and up

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.840
the bank, so friction acts down the bank: inward and downward.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.020
At the upper limiting speed its magnitude is mu times N.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.180
The inward equation is N sine theta plus mu N cosine theta equals m v squared

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:46.780
over r.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.210
The vertical equation is N cosine theta minus mu N sine theta equals m

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:54.320
g.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.120
Notice that N is not m g.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.680
Divide the first equation by the second.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.800
After dividing numerator and denominator by cosine theta, v squared over r g

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.280
becomes tangent theta plus mu, divided by one minus mu tangent theta.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:13.280
Let's use tangent theta equal to three quarters, mu equal to one quarter,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.440
radius twenty five meters, and g equal to ten.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.500
The ratio is one divided by thirteen sixteenths, or sixteen thirteenths.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.980
Therefore maximum speed squared is four thousand over thirteen, and maximum

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:30.240
speed is approximately seventeen point five four meters per second.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:32.940
Pause before calling that answer valid.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.700
The denominator is positive, so the vertical balance permits a positive normal

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.040
force at this limiting state.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.820
If the denominator were zero or negative, do not take a square root of an

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.420
invalid expression and call it a real speed boundary.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.420
Return to the friction inequality and contact assumptions.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.740
Our formula is a model with a domain, not a universal rule.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.400
For this numerical case, the normal reaction divided by m g at the upper

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.920
boundary is twenty over thirteen.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.871
Friction has a downward component, so the normal's upward component must

00:02:06.871 --> 00:02:07.200
supply

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:07.940
more than the weight.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.040
That check helps explain why simply setting N equal to m g would fail.

00:02:13.152 --> 00:02:14.652
Now reduce the speed.

00:02:15.392 --> 00:02:19.072
Below the frictionless value, the car tends to slip inward and down the bank.

00:02:20.032 --> 00:02:24.012
Static friction reverses: it points up the bank, outward and upward.

00:02:24.752 --> 00:02:29.852
At a positive lower limiting speed, the inward equation is N sine theta minus

00:02:29.852 --> 00:02:33.352
mu N cosine theta equals m v squared over r.

00:02:33.952 --> 00:02:39.652
The vertical equation is N cosine theta plus mu N sine theta equals m g.

00:02:39.652 --> 00:02:40.512
Divide them carefully.

00:02:41.192 --> 00:02:45.952
The lower speed squared is r g times tangent theta minus mu, divided by one

00:02:45.952 --> 00:02:47.092
plus mu tangent theta.

00:02:47.972 --> 00:02:52.914
Use our same twenty five meter radius, g ten, tangent theta three quarters,

00:02:52.914 --> 00:02:53.112
and

00:02:53.112 --> 00:02:53.712
mu one quarter.

00:02:54.392 --> 00:02:58.812
The ratio is one half divided by nineteen sixteenths, or eight nineteenths.

00:02:59.452 --> 00:03:03.692
Minimum speed squared is two thousand over nineteen, so minimum speed is about

00:03:03.692 --> 00:03:05.472
ten point two six meters per second.

00:03:06.032 --> 00:03:09.392
The frictionless speed is the square root of one hundred eighty seven point

00:03:09.392 --> 00:03:12.432
five, about thirteen point six nine.

00:03:12.432 --> 00:03:15.192
It lies between the two limits, exactly as we hoped.

00:03:15.892 --> 00:03:20.355
At that middle speed, friction can be zero; static friction is not always mu

00:03:20.355 --> 00:03:20.472
N.

00:03:21.132 --> 00:03:22.752
What if the coefficient were one instead?

00:03:23.132 --> 00:03:26.212
The expression for the lower squared speed becomes negative.

00:03:26.672 --> 00:03:28.972
That does not create an imaginary physical speed.

00:03:29.512 --> 00:03:33.532
At rest, force balance on the slope requires friction divided by normal

00:03:33.532 --> 00:03:35.452
reaction equal to tangent theta.

00:03:36.072 --> 00:03:40.076
Our tangent is three quarters, less than the available coefficient one, so

00:03:40.076 --> 00:03:40.292
rest

00:03:40.292 --> 00:03:42.972
is possible and the physical lower speed is zero.

00:03:43.612 --> 00:03:47.747
For the general check, solve for the signed required friction f directed up

00:03:47.747 --> 00:03:47.912
the

00:03:47.912 --> 00:03:48.112
bank.

00:03:48.712 --> 00:03:53.452
Its ratio to N is tangent theta minus q, divided by one plus q tangent theta,

00:03:53.892 --> 00:03:56.432
where q is v squared over r g.

00:03:56.432 --> 00:03:59.272
Demand the absolute value of that ratio be no greater than mu.

00:03:59.732 --> 00:04:02.292
That single inequality checks either friction direction.

00:04:02.852 --> 00:04:06.240
For example, at ten meters per second in our original quarter coefficient

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:06.472
case,

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:08.092
q is zero point four.

00:04:08.772 --> 00:04:13.092
Required friction divided by N is about zero point two six nine, above one

00:04:13.092 --> 00:04:15.012
quarter, so that speed is not allowed.

00:04:15.632 --> 00:04:20.492
At fourteen meters per second it is about minus zero point zero two one four,

00:04:20.492 --> 00:04:21.312
safely within the bound.

00:04:21.612 --> 00:04:24.232
The negative sign means friction acts down the bank.

00:04:25.012 --> 00:04:28.925
State the model, resolve actual forces, and test the inequality before

00:04:28.925 --> 00:04:29.372
trusting

00:04:29.372 --> 00:04:31.012
a memorized maximum or minimum.
